#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087462 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d21d6066-f7c8-5a47-b150-d0ae25d2d60b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given an Inhomogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "853bdfff-d043-58d0-bcb7-f1f5891eb31c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5216" :question/problem "Find the center and the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $25x^2 + 9y^2 - 100x - 18y = 116$." :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25x^{2} + 9y^{2} - 100x - 18y &= 116 \\\\\n(25x^{2} - 100x) + (9y^{2} - 18y) &= 116\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(25x^{2} - 100x) + (9y^{2} - 18y) &= 116 \\\\\n25[x^{2} - 4x] + 9[y^{2} - 2y] &= 116 \\\\\n25[(x - 2)^{2} - 4] + 9[(y - 1)^{2} - 1] &= 116 \\\\\n25(x - 2)^{2} - 100 + 9(y - 1)^{2} - 9 &= 116\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25(x - 2)^{2} - 100 + 9(y - 1)^{2} - 9 &= 116 \\\\\n25(x - 2)^{2} + 9(y - 1)^{2} &= 116 + 100 + 9 \\\\\n25(x - 2)^{2} + 9(y - 1)^{2} &= 225 \\\\\n((x - 2)^{2})/(9) + ((y - 1)^{2})/(25) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 2)^{2})/(3^{2}) + ((y - 1)^{2})/(5^{2}) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5 > 3$, the length of the major axis is\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 5 =10\n$$\n\nthe length of the minor axis is\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 3 =6\n$$\n\nand the ellipse is centered at $({2},{1})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087463 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d58fba3c-4bbf-5a74-a911-18db101eb632" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given a Homogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "0b3df81e-03a7-5cf2-b815-f999012b9b2b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5217" :question/problem "Find the center and the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $x^2 + 4y^2 - 6x + 24y + 41 = 0$." :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 4y^{2} - 6x + 24y + 41 &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 6x) + (4y^{2} + 24y) + 41 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{2} - 6x) + (4y^{2} + 24y) + 41 &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 6x) + 4[y^{2} + 6y] + 41 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} - 9 + 4[(y + 3)^{2} - 9] + 41 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} - 9 + 4(y + 3)^{2} - 36 + 41 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 3)^{2} - 9 + 4(y + 3)^{2} - 36 + 41 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} + 4(y + 3)^{2} &= 9 + 36 - 41 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} + 4(y + 3)^{2} &= 4 \\\\\n((x - 3)^{2})/(4) + ((y + 3)^{2})/(1) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 3)^{2})/(2^{2}) + ((y + 3)^{2})/(1^{2}) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2 > 1$, the length of the major axis is\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 2 =4\n$$\n\nthe length of the minor axis is\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 1 =2\n$$\n\nand the ellipse is centered at $({3},{-3})$."}]}
